"Arranges pairwise scatterplots in a matrix to reveal relationships among several quantitative variables.\n\nUse when: A small set of numeric variables needs broad pairwise inspection. Outliers, nonlinear patterns, and marginal distributions should be visible together.\n\nAvoid when: Too many variables or observations make individual panels too small or dense.\n\nVisual grammar: Each panel’s axes encode one variable pair. Repeated point identity, optional group color, and diagonal distributions connect the matrix views.\n\nBuild: Select a focused set of variables and consistent group encodings. Arrange all variable pairs with matching scales for repeated variables. Use diagonal histograms or labels and keep outer axis labels concise.\n\nCheck: Check each row and column consistently refer to their named variable. Verify matching variable scales across all relevant panels. Confirm pairwise missing-data rules and sample sizes are disclosed.\n\nPitfalls: The duplicated upper and lower triangles can waste space; use one triangle when sufficient. Correlation impressions can be driven by outliers or groups, so inspect rather than infer causation.\n\nExample: Inspect synthetic vehicle price, weight, efficiency, and range with one scatterplot per pair and diagonal distributions.\n\nAlternatives: Scatterplot, Correlation matrix, Parallel coordinates."
